<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Khwarazm</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 305 CE (approx.) · capital Kath</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The ancient oasis kingdom of the lower Amu Darya, a land of canals and fortified towns that kept its own kings and Iranian culture for eight centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, or Chorasmia, grew from irrigation farming on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> delta south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>. It appears as a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> and seems to have regained independence by the 4th century BCE, when its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharasmanes-of-khwarazm">Pharasmanes</a> offered alliance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 328 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its economy rested on a dense canal network feeding wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, vines and orchards, protected by walled settlements and fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toprak-kala">Toprak-Kala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koy-krylgan-kala">Koy-Krylgan-Kala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayaz-kala">Ayaz-Kala</a>. Kings issued their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coinage, and later Khwarazmian tradition traced their line to the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyavash">Siyavush</a>. Zoroastrian practice included bone burial in ossuaries, and the Khwarazmian language had its own Aramaic-derived script.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 4th century CE the ancient royal house gave way to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/afrighid-khwarazm">Afrighid Khwarazm</a> dynasty, which al-Biruni dates to 305. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> may have held loose overlordship at times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm's</a> distinct language and calendar survived until the Mongol era.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 500 BCE – 305 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:49.75%;width:16.02%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 305 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kath</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pharasmanes</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khwarazmian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chorasmia, Ancient Khwarazm, Siyavushid Khwarazm</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-98&amp;focus=kingdom-of-khwarazm" aria-label="Kingdom of Khwarazm on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6149 693 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Khwarazm lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6469 949L6140 1251M6469 949L6757 1168M6469 949L6480 949"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M6757 1168h0M6480 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6469 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6469 949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kath. <a href="/atlas?year=-98&amp;focus=